A||||7||duke||Bearing clearances||||1||
Z||000000||duke||11-27-2001||11:00 PM||||Will poor main and rod bearing clearances cause low oil pressure? How does the oil flow through the 2.5L Duke?||||reg||1
Z||000001||cadaver||11-28-2001||12:35 AM||||Yes excessive clearences will lower the oil pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||11-28-2001||01:03 AM||||Go and buy some plastigague.  Then check your clearences for exact measurements.  Yes a engine with larger clearences will run with lower oil pressure.  If you run a higher weight oil that MAY make up for the worn bearings.  I am not sure about the specs of the duke but generally I like about .002 more loose than the maximum spec for an engine rebuild, that is personal preference though.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||duke||11-28-2001||11:00 PM||||Thanks. I had my crank ground .010" and bought some .010" oversize bearings but when I installed them, I couldn't even turn the crank. I measured the journals and it looks like they were ground the .010" but the bearings are just too tight. Are there shims in .001" increments that I can install under the caps to get the clearance I need? Can I use a standard size for one half and the .010 for the other half if the clearance is within spec? I would appreciate any suggestions...||||reg||8
Z||000004||Hank is Here||11-29-2001||02:10 AM||||There are no shims that can fit under the bearings, shims that fit under the bearings would make the fit tighter if anything.  Get plastigauge and see what the actual clearence is!  Make sure you do this test dry and also be sure to wipe off the protective haze off of the actual bearing surface.  <BR>Also you need to specify what was ground .010 was it the main or the rods?  <BR>I doubt there is any bearinbg made for the Fiero in odd number of thousandts, my one jeep motor I have ground .010 and put .015 bearings in it.  That motor was the exception rather than the rule though. <P>Have you ever noticed that new motors don't get as goood gas milage as slightly used ones...that is due partially to the tightness of the bearings.  <P><BR>Hank  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||11-29-2001||02:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by duke:<BR><B>Thanks. I had my crank ground .010" and bought some .010" oversize bearings but when I installed them, I couldn't even turn the crank. I measured the journals and it looks like they were ground the .010" but the bearings are just too tight. Are there shims in .001" increments that I can install under the caps to get the clearance I need? Can I use a standard size for one half and the .010 for the other half if the clearance is within spec? I would appreciate any suggestions...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You couldn't shim the end cap, as this would create an oblong/oval hole instead of a circle.  Think about it...you're just making the circle wider.  If this did solve your problem, it would be VERY temporary..just long enough for you to start it up and hear a funny noise that developed into a rod knock within a few miles.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||duke||11-29-2001||11:56 AM||||The main journals were ground the .010". I know the .010 oversize bearing are too tight, so I will install standard size bearings and use the plastigage to see what the clearance is. Then, I will install one half of the .010 and the other half standard and see what I get.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Nashco||11-29-2001||12:36 PM||||Again, remember you must keep a perfect (or very very close to it) circle with the bearings.  Mixing and matching bearing halves would create an imperfect circle.  The only way to fix your problem is with a different thickness SET of bearings or grinding the crank a little more.<P>Use the plastiguage with a new set of stock bearings to see what your clearances are.  Let us know what you find out.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
